        "original_id": "3f74251a-c808-5e5e-8c2d-9779ba6b689a",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: R. T. Rybak, Dan Cohen\n- Events: Minneapolis mayoral election, 2009; Minneapolis mayoral election, 2013\n- Places: Minneapolis\n- Roles: Mayor of Minneapolis\n\nFacts:\n- R. T. Rybak took office as Mayor of Minneapolis in January 2002.\n- R. T. Rybak won a second term as Mayor of Minneapolis in November 2005.\n- The 2009 Minneapolis mayoral election was held in Minneapolis on November 3, 2009 to elect the Mayor of Minneapolis for a four-year term.\n- R. T. Rybak won a third term in the 2009 Minneapolis mayoral election, winning re-election in the first round with 73.6% of the vote.\n- In late 2012, R. T. Rybak announced that he would not seek a fourth term as Mayor of Minneapolis.\n- After R. T. Rybak announced he would not run again, 35 candidates began campaigns in the 2013 Minneapolis mayoral election.\n- Dan Cohen ran as an independent candidate in the 2013 Minneapolis mayoral election and finished seventh out of 35 candidates.\n- Dan Cohen has provided financial support to candidates of the Democratic and Republican parties.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "884828e9-2697-5c14-8213-91d7416937c4",
        "text": "Page: Minneapolis mayoral election, 2009. Sentence: The 2009 Minneapolis mayoral election was held on November 3, 2009 to elect the Mayor of Minneapolis for a four-year term.\nPage: Minneapolis mayoral election, 2009. Sentence: Incumbent R. T. Rybak won re-election for a third term in the first round with 73.6% of the vote.\nPage: R. T. Rybak. Sentence: He took office in January 2002, and won a second term in November 2005 and a third in November 2009.\nPage: Minneapolis mayoral election, 2013. Sentence: After incumbent Mayor R. T. Rybak announced in late 2012 that he would not seek a fourth term, 35 candidates began campaigns to replace him.\nPage: Dan Cohen. Sentence: He has provided financial support to candidates of the Democratic and Republican parties and ran as an independent candidate in the 2013 Minneapolis mayoral election, ultimately finishing seventh out of 35 candidates.",
